"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[39257],{239257:n=>{n.exports="# Schema & DDL Migration\n\nSource dialect → DBSQL equivalents for schema objects, table definitions, indexes, and constraints.\n\n> **Databricks defaults**: All managed tables are **Delta format** by default — no `USING DELTA` clause is required unless overriding. Reference permanent objects using **Unity Catalog three-part names**: `catalog.schema.table`.\n\n## MSSQL\n\n### Identity Columns\n\n> DBSQL identity column DDL is shown inline below. Load `skills/writing-sql/table-optimization.md` if you need clustering or partitioning context alongside DDL changes.\n\n**WARNING: Identity columns have significant implications in Databricks.**\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL\nCREATE TABLE t (\n    id INT IDENTITY(1,1) PRIMARY KEY,\n    name VARCHAR(100)\n);\n\n-- DBSQL (Delta, Unity Catalog)\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.t (\n    id BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY,\n    name STRING\n);\n```\n\n**Critical limitations:**\n\n1. **Disables parallel writes** — Tables with identity columns cannot be written concurrently\n2. **Values not guaranteed sequential** — May have gaps; not in insertion order\n3. **Cannot be added later** — Cannot add identity columns via ALTER TABLE\n4. **No SCOPE_IDENTITY()** — No DBSQL equivalent\n\n**Preferred alternative — UUID (allows parallel writes):**\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.t (id STRING DEFAULT UUID(), name STRING);\n```\n\nIf a numeric surrogate key is required and writes are strictly single-threaded, use `ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY ...) + COALESCE(MAX(id), 0)` from a staging MERGE. **This pattern breaks under concurrent writes** (both writers compute the same MAX and produce duplicate IDs) — only use when you can guarantee no parallel ingestion.\n\n### Indexes and Constraints\n\nT-SQL: `CREATE INDEX`, `PRIMARY KEY`, `UNIQUE`, `CHECK`, `FOREIGN KEY`\n\nDBSQL: Indexes not supported (Delta uses data skipping + Z-ORDER). Primary keys, foreign keys, and unique constraints exist as **informational only** (not enforced). CHECK constraints **ARE enforced on write** — behavior matches T-SQL.\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL: enforced foreign key\nALTER TABLE orders ADD CONSTRAINT fk_customer FOREIGN KEY (customer_id) REFERENCES customers(id);\n\n-- DBSQL: informational only (not enforced); use UC three-part names\nALTER TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.orders ADD CONSTRAINT fk_customer\n  FOREIGN KEY (customer_id) REFERENCES my_catalog.my_schema.customers(id) NOT ENFORCED;\n\n-- T-SQL: CHECK constraint (enforced)\nALTER TABLE orders ADD CONSTRAINT chk_amount CHECK (amount > 0);\n\n-- DBSQL: CHECK constraint (also enforced — behavior is the same)\nALTER TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.orders ADD CONSTRAINT chk_amount CHECK (amount > 0);\n```\n\n### ALTER TABLE — column changes\n\nDBSQL has restrictions on column modifications that T-SQL does not:\n\n| T-SQL                                          | DBSQL                                                                        | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n| ---------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `ALTER TABLE t ADD col INT`                    | `ALTER TABLE t ADD COLUMN col INT`                                           | Same intent; DBSQL requires `COLUMN` keyword                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n| `ALTER TABLE t ADD col INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0` | `ALTER TABLE t ADD COLUMN col INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0`                        | Delta accepts NOT NULL with a DEFAULT for new columns                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n| `ALTER TABLE t ALTER COLUMN col BIGINT`        | `ALTER TABLE t ALTER COLUMN col TYPE BIGINT` (type widening only, DBR 14.3+) | Delta supports safe type promotions (INT→BIGINT, FLOAT→DOUBLE, etc.) when the table property `delta.enableTypeWidening = true` is set. Type narrowing and incompatible type changes still require drop-and-recreate or query-time `CAST`. |\n| `ALTER TABLE t DROP COLUMN col`                | `ALTER TABLE t DROP COLUMN col`                                              | Supported in Delta; Delta files retain the column data until `REORG TABLE ... APPLY (PURGE)` is run                                                                                                                                       |\n| `ALTER TABLE t RENAME COLUMN old TO new`       | `ALTER TABLE t RENAME COLUMN old TO new`                                     | Supported                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL: change column type (common migration pattern — widening VARCHAR)\nALTER TABLE orders ALTER COLUMN notes NVARCHAR(MAX);\n\n-- DBSQL option 1: type widening for safe promotions (DBR 14.3+ with enableTypeWidening)\n-- First enable widening on the table (one-time):\nALTER TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.orders SET TBLPROPERTIES ('delta.enableTypeWidening' = 'true');\n-- Then widen the column (INT→BIGINT, FLOAT→DOUBLE, etc.):\nALTER TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.orders ALTER COLUMN notes TYPE STRING;\n\n-- DBSQL option 2: cast at query time (non-destructive, any version)\nSELECT CAST(notes AS STRING) AS notes FROM my_catalog.my_schema.orders;\n-- DBSQL option 3: recreate the table with the new type (destructive — requires data copy)\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.orders_new AS SELECT *, CAST(notes AS STRING) AS notes FROM my_catalog.my_schema.orders;\n```\n\n### DEFAULT Constraints\n\nT-SQL `DEFAULT` constraints map directly to `DEFAULT` expressions in DBSQL column definitions. The functions differ:\n\n| T-SQL default expression   | DBSQL equivalent              |\n| -------------------------- | ----------------------------- |\n| `DEFAULT GETDATE()`        | `DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP`   |\n| `DEFAULT NEWID()`          | `DEFAULT UUID()`              |\n| `DEFAULT 0` / `DEFAULT ''` | Same — literals are identical |\n| `DEFAULT GETUTCDATE()`     | `DEFAULT UTC_TIMESTAMP()`     |\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL\nCREATE TABLE orders (\n    order_id   INT          IDENTITY(1,1),\n    created_at DATETIME     DEFAULT GETDATE(),\n    status     VARCHAR(20)  DEFAULT 'pending',\n    is_active  BIT          DEFAULT 1\n);\n\n-- DBSQL\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.orders (\n    order_id   BIGINT     GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY,\n    created_at TIMESTAMP  DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n    status     STRING     DEFAULT 'pending',\n    is_active  BOOLEAN    DEFAULT true\n);\n```\n\nNamed T-SQL `DEFAULT` constraints (`CONSTRAINT df_status DEFAULT 'pending'`) have no named-constraint equivalent in DBSQL — translate to an inline `DEFAULT` expression and drop the constraint name.\n\n### Computed Columns\n\nT-SQL `AS (expression)` computed columns have **no direct DBSQL equivalent**. The column is evaluated on every read in T-SQL; DBSQL does not support this construct.\n\n**Migration options:**\n\n1. **Generated column via CTAS** — if the expression is pure and the table is built from a SELECT, include the computed column in the `AS SELECT` list.\n2. **View** — create a view over the base table that adds the computed expression as a derived column. Callers reference the view instead of the table.\n3. **Write-time computation** — evaluate the expression in the INSERT/MERGE and store the result as a regular column. Requires updating all write paths.\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL\nCREATE TABLE order_lines (\n    quantity    INT,\n    unit_price  DECIMAL(10,2),\n    line_total  AS (quantity * unit_price)  -- computed, not stored\n);\n\n-- DBSQL option 1: view (read-transparent replacement)\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.order_lines (\n    quantity   INT,\n    unit_price DECIMAL(10,2)\n    -- line_total removed; computed in view below\n);\n\nCREATE OR REPLACE VIEW my_catalog.my_schema.order_lines_v AS\nSELECT *, quantity * unit_price AS line_total\nFROM my_catalog.my_schema.order_lines;\n\n-- DBSQL option 2: store computed value at write time (if write paths are known)\nINSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.order_lines (quantity, unit_price, line_total)\nVALUES (5, 9.99, 5 * 9.99);\n```\n\n**PERSISTED computed columns** (T-SQL `AS (...) PERSISTED`) — stored physically on disk. Migrate as a regular column with write-time computation (option 2 above); they have no lazy-eval characteristic to preserve.\n\n### SEQUENCE objects\n\nT-SQL `CREATE SEQUENCE` / `NEXT VALUE FOR` has a direct DBSQL equivalent:\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL\nCREATE SEQUENCE dbo.order_seq\n    START WITH 1000\n    INCREMENT BY 1\n    MINVALUE 1000\n    MAXVALUE 9999999\n    NO CYCLE;\n\nSELECT NEXT VALUE FOR dbo.order_seq;\n\n-- DBSQL (Unity Catalog three-part name)\nCREATE SEQUENCE my_catalog.my_schema.order_seq\n    START WITH 1000\n    INCREMENT BY 1\n    MINVALUE 1000\n    MAXVALUE 9999999;  -- NO CYCLE is the default\n\nSELECT NEXT VALUE FOR my_catalog.my_schema.order_seq;\n```\n\n**Differences:**\n\n- `NO CYCLE` is the default in DBSQL — omit the clause\n- `CYCLE` is supported in DBSQL if the source uses it\n- `CACHE` option is not supported in DBSQL — remove it\n- `OWNED BY` (PostgreSQL-style) is not supported — remove it\n- Sequences in DBSQL are available on SQL warehouses and Databricks Runtime 13.0+\n\n## SYNAPSE\n\nSame as MSSQL plus:\n\n**DISTRIBUTION clause** — Synapse table distribution options have no DBSQL equivalent:\n\n```sql\n-- Synapse\nCREATE TABLE fact_sales (...) WITH (DISTRIBUTION = HASH(customer_id), CLUSTERED COLUMNSTORE INDEX);\n\n-- DBSQL (Delta is default; columnar storage is automatic; use liquid clustering instead of DISTRIBUTION)\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.fact_sales (...) CLUSTER BY (customer_id);\n```\n\n**CREATE TABLE AS SELECT (CTAS)**:\n\n```sql\n-- Synapse\nCREATE TABLE agg_sales WITH (DISTRIBUTION = ROUND_ROBIN) AS SELECT ...;\n\n-- DBSQL\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.agg_sales AS SELECT ...;\n```\n"}}]);
